articles.scope:
Covers the AZ-900 benefit pairs and separates them:
- reliability from predictability (performance versus cost predictability);
- governance in the cloud (policy, templates, compliance) from security;
- management of the cloud (autoscale, monitoring) from management in the cloud (portal, CLI, APIs).
Maps each benefit to the AWS value proposition (economies of scale, agility, global reach) and to Well-Architected pillars, linking architecture/well-architected for depth.

Compares the pricing options:
- on-demand pricing;
- reservations versus savings plans (Azure savings plan for compute and AWS Compute Savings Plans are flexible; EC2 Instance Savings Plans are family- and region-bound);
- Spot and BYOL;
- free offers (Azure free account versus the July 2025 AWS Free Tier plans).
Covers the factors that drive cost (egress, region, licensing, support) and TCO evaluation. States the exception that AWS zonal Reserved Instances also reserve capacity. Purchasing mechanics stay in cloud/azure/cost and cloud/aws/cost.

Teaches the neutral model shared by VNets and VPCs:
- CIDR and non-overlapping address plans, segmentation, and public versus private endpoints;
- BGP, NAT/SNAT and network virtual appliances;
- L4 versus L7 load balancing and CDN;
- hub-and-spoke, and VPN versus dedicated circuits.
Highlights the key structural difference: Azure subnets are regional, AWS subnets are AZ-scoped.

Covers:
- member versus guest users;
- security versus Microsoft 365 groups, assigned versus dynamic membership, and role-assignable groups;
- group-based licensing and bulk operations;
- custom security attributes;
- SSPR with writeback;
- disabling accounts and revoking sessions.
Shows delegation with administrative units (including restricted management AUs) and automation with Microsoft Graph in C# and Python.

Teaches Conditional Access policy design:
- policy anatomy, templates, report-only rollout and testing;
- authentication strength and authentication context, continuous access evaluation, protected actions, session controls and device-based controls;
- risk-based policies from ID Protection for users, sign-ins and workload identities.
States that CA for workload identities needs Workload ID Premium, and that extending CA and ID Protection to agents requires Microsoft Agent 365. AWS has no direct equivalent: IAM condition keys and Verified Access are partial.

Configures enterprise applications:
- app-level and tenant-level settings, gallery and custom apps;
- SSO modes: SAML, OIDC, password-based and linked;
- user and group assignment, app roles and SCIM provisioning;
- My Apps and application collections;
- delegated app-management roles and consent.
Protocol theory stays in security/iam; header-based on-premises apps are covered in the Global Secure Access article.

Configures federated identity credentials on app registrations or UAMIs, covering:
- case-sensitive issuer, subject and audience matching and the 20-credential limit;
- flexible FICs (preview): claims-matching expressions for GitHub, GitLab and Terraform Cloud, set via Graph or az rest because CLI, PowerShell and Terraform don't support them yet;
- GitHub's immutable subject format for new, renamed and transferred repositories from July 15, 2026.
Includes Bicep and Terraform. The AWS-side IAM outbound identity federation setup is in cloud/aws/identity.

Teaches just-in-time access with PIM:
- eligible versus active and time-bound assignments, activation controls, approvals and audit history;
- PIM for Groups and Azure resource roles;
- emergency access accounts, which need passkeys or CBA under mandatory MFA.
Covers access reviews, entitlement management (catalogs, access packages, terms of use, connected organizations, external user lifecycle) and lifecycle workflows, each with its license boundary.

Compares:
- guest B2B collaboration (external collaboration settings, bulk invites, SAML/WS-Fed IdPs) with B2B direct connect;
- cross-tenant access settings and synchronization, and multitenant organizations;
- CIAM in external tenants with legacy Azure AD B2C (no new sales since May 1, 2025).
Notes decentralized identity with Verified ID. Maps CIAM to Amazon Cognito and partner access to cross-account roles.

Explains Microsoft's Security Service Edge (SSE):
- traffic forwarding profiles and Global Secure Access clients;
- Private Access as per-app ZTNA;
- Internet Access as a secure web gateway, plus Internet Access for Microsoft services;
- application proxy for on-premises web apps;
- the shared, outbound-only Microsoft Entra private network connector.
Contrasts with P2S VPN and AWS Verified Access, and links security/network-security for the ZTNA/SSE concepts.

Explains:
- the root management group plus up to six levels below it;
- subscriptions as billing, quota and policy boundaries, and resource groups as lifecycle containers;
- Service Groups (public preview): tenant-level, up to 10 nesting levels, multiple membership, RBAC inheriting only to child service groups, no access to or policy over members;
- tag rules (50 pairs, no default inheritance), with Cost Management tag inheritance versus Policy modify inheritance.
Ends with a hierarchy design method.

Covers:
- definition structure and modes, initiatives, assignment scope and notScopes, enforcement mode and exemptions;
- all 11 effects in Learn's evaluation order: disabled, append/modify, deny, audit, manual, auditIfNotExists, denyAction, with auditIfNotExists and deployIfNotExists running again after the resource provider succeeds;
- remediation tasks, regulatory compliance and Machine Configuration.
Contrasts with SCPs, RCPs, AWS Organizations declarative policies and AWS Config rules.

Builds a policy-as-code practice:
- policy libraries (ALZ Library) and Enterprise Policy as Code;
- GitHub Actions or Azure Pipelines deployment using workload identity federation;
- versioned built-ins, testing and compliance scans;
- exemption expiry governance, drift handling and Machine Configuration packages.
Contrasts with SCP/RCP pipelines, Config conformance packs and Control Tower controls.

Covers subscription lifecycle:
- creating subscriptions programmatically (subscription alias API for EA/MCA), renaming, and moving between management groups;
- transferring billing ownership, and subscription policies that block entering or leaving a tenant;
- canceling and reactivating, and moving resources between resource groups and subscriptions.
Warns that a directory change removes RBAC assignments and custom roles and breaks managed identities and Key Vault tenant bindings. Shows C# and Python samples and the AWS Organizations equivalents.

Teaches the current reference architecture:
- the Platform landing zone with Security, Management, Identity and Connectivity;
- workload landing zones under Internal ('Corp'), Online and Local (Azure Local clusters);
- Sandbox and Decommissioned.
Covers design areas, hub-spoke versus Virtual WAN, the IaC accelerator with Azure Verified Modules (Bicep/Terraform), the portal accelerator, subscription vending and the Sovereign Landing Zone variant.

Uses the reader's .NET 9 and Angular stack to walk the AZ-305 scenario path:
- vending, management group placement and policy;
- managed identities;
- hub-spoke peering, private endpoints with central DNS, and explicit egress;
- Front Door with WAF, and monitoring.
Records each decision as a linked ADR and shows the AWS-equivalent path.

Covers:
- VNet and subnet planning: reserved addresses, segmentation, dedicated versus shared subnets and delegation;
- private subnets as the default for VNets created with API versions released after March 31, 2026;
- public IPs: SKUs (Basic retired, Standard, StandardV2), regional and global tiers, routing preference, prefixes and custom IP prefixes (BYOIP);
- IPv6 dual-stack, virtual network encryption and AVNM IPAM pools.
Contrasts AZ-scoped AWS subnets with regional Azure subnets.

Covers:
- SKUs (Basic is S2S VPN only) and virtual hub architecture;
- VPN, P2S and ExpressRoute gateways with scale units;
- hub routing: route tables, association, propagation and labels;
- routing intent and secured hubs with Azure Firewall;
- third-party NVAs and SaaS in the hub, and Virtual WAN security settings.
Maps to AWS Transit Gateway route tables and Cloud WAN segments.

Hands-on configuration:
- S2S: local network gateways, connections, custom IPsec/IKE policies, BGP and active-active;
- P2S: tunnel types, certificate, RADIUS or Entra ID authentication, and client configuration files for Azure VPN Client;
- Always On VPN and Azure Network Adapter requirements, and Azure Extended Network subnet stretch;
- VPN troubleshoot and client diagnostics.
Includes Bicep and Terraform.

Covers:
- VNet DNS settings and Azure-provided DNS (168.63.129.16);
- public zones, alias records, and DNSSEC signing (ECDSAP256SHA256, RFC 9824); the Azure resolver doesn't validate DNSSEC;
- private zones with VNet links and autoregistration;
- Private Resolver inbound and outbound endpoints with forwarding rulesets;
- DNS resolver policy (formerly DNS security policy).
Maps to Route 53 hosted zones, Resolver endpoints and DNS Firewall.

Contrasts the PaaS access options:
- private endpoints and Private Link service;
- basic service endpoints and standard service endpoints with network identifiers (GA per the dedicated page; a dated callout notes Learn's inconsistencies);
- service endpoint policies and PaaS firewalls, including Storage;
- network security perimeter (GA; transition versus enforced mode). Cosmos DB, SQL DB, App Configuration, Azure OpenAI and Event Grid are preview with NSP; Sentinel workspaces and Azure Backup storage aren't supported.
Maps to AWS interface, gateway and resource endpoints.

Places services on two axes, L4 versus L7 and regional versus global:
- Standard Load Balancer (Regional and Global tiers) and Gateway Load Balancer;
- Application Gateway v2 (Standard_v2, WAF_v2, TCP/TLS proxy) and Basic (preview: 99.9% SLA, no TCP/TLS proxy, mTLS, Private Link or AGIC); v1 retired April 28, 2026;
- Front Door Standard and Premium; classic has had no new profiles since April 1, 2025 and retires March 31, 2027;
- Azure CDN from Microsoft (classic): no new resources since August 15, 2025, retires September 30, 2027;
- Traffic Manager.
Maps each to AWS.

Hands-on configuration:
- Load Balancer: frontends, pools, rules, probes, HA ports, inbound NAT and outbound rules;
- Gateway Load Balancer chaining;
- Traffic Manager profiles, routing methods, nested profiles and endpoint monitoring;
- Application Gateway: listeners, rules, backend settings (the exam's 'HTTP settings'), TLS, rewrites and autoscale;
- Front Door routes, origins, caching, acceleration, rules and Private Link origins.
Includes Bicep, Terraform and a troubleshooting checklist.

Covers:
- SKUs: Basic, Standard, and Premium with TLS inspection, IDPS and URL/web categories;
- DNAT, network and application rules with collection groups, FQDN and service tags;
- DNS proxy, threat intelligence, and forced tunneling with a management NIC;
- NAT gateway integration for SNAT scale;
- Firewall Manager parent and child policies, and secured virtual hubs versus hub VNets.
Maps to AWS Network Firewall and Firewall Manager.

Compares WAF on Front Door and on Application Gateway:
- policy scopes and association;
- managed rule sets (Microsoft Default Rule Set, OWASP CRS) and Bot Manager rule sets;
- detection versus prevention mode, anomaly scoring and exclusions;
- custom, geo and rate-limit rules, and logging and tuning.
Maps to AWS WAF.

Compares Bastion tiers:
- Developer: no dedicated subnet;
- Basic: two instances;
- Standard: host scaling, native client, shareable links, custom ports;
- Premium: session recording, private-only deployment.
Covers the /26+ AzureBastionSubnet, the Standard static public IP and the NSG rules administration requires. Zone support is preview in some regions. Contrasts with JIT VM access, Entra Private Access, and AWS Session Manager or EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint.

A diagnostic playbook using Azure Monitor Network Insights (formerly Azure Monitor for Networks), Connection monitor, IP flow verify, NSG diagnostics, next hop, effective rules, packet capture and VPN troubleshoot. VNet flow logs:
- record NSG and AVNM security admin decisions and encryption state;
- need a Standard storage account in the same region;
- don't support App Service, Functions, ACI, Container Apps, SQL MI and other services.
NSG flow logs accept no new creation and retire September 30, 2027. Maps to VPC Flow Logs and Reachability Analyzer.

Covers the performance levers:
- accelerated networking and VM bandwidth limits;
- proximity placement and inter-zone latency;
- routing preference (Microsoft network versus internet);
- Front Door acceleration and ExpressRoute FastPath;
- measuring latency with Connection monitor and tools such as latte.
Maps to AWS ENA, placement groups, Global Accelerator and Direct Connect SiteLink.

Integrates the component articles into one design:
- hub Azure Firewall Premium, spoke UDRs and forced tunneling;
- NAT gateway on the firewall subnet;
- central private DNS with Private Resolver;
- Bastion, AVNM security admin guardrails and NSP for PaaS;
- edge WAF and VNet flow logs to the SIEM.
Includes failure and scale analysis, mapped to an AWS centralized inspection VPC with Transit Gateway and Network Firewall.

Covers:
- required VM resources, size naming and Gen1 versus Gen2;
- Marketplace images, Compute Gallery and VM Image Builder;
- extensions, cloud-init and Run Command;
- security types: Standard, Trusted Launch (Secure Boot, vTPM, boot integrity monitoring) and Confidential VM.
Portal, CLI and PowerShell default Gen2 VMs to Trusted Launch. ARM, Bicep, Terraform and SDK deployments default to it with Compute API 2025-11-01+ when supported; securityType Standard opts out. Managed images aren't supported.

Compares:
- Recovery Services versus Backup vaults and backup policies;
- restore options, including cross-region restore;
- Site Recovery zone-to-zone and region-to-region replication, test failover, failover and failback;
- security features: soft delete, immutability, multi-user authorization, CMK and private endpoints;
- backup reports and alerts.
Data-service backups stay in storage-data. Maps to AWS Backup and Elastic Disaster Recovery.

Turns recovery objectives into compute designs:
- zone-redundant scale sets and region pairs versus chosen secondaries;
- Site Recovery zone-to-zone versus region-to-region;
- backup tiers and DR drills.
Covers hybrid workloads. RTO/RPO theory stays in architecture/reliability; the AWS mapping is multi-AZ Auto Scaling plus Elastic Disaster Recovery.
